,_Inc.)\n* [Edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit \"Edit the source code of this page [e]\")\n* [View history](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=history \"Past revisions of this page [h]\")\n\n\n\nTools\n\nActions\n\n* [Read](/wiki/F5,_Inc.)\n* [Edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit \"Edit the source code of this page [e]\")\n* [View history](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=history \"Past revisions of this page [h]\")\n\nGeneral\n\n* [What links here](/wiki/Special:WhatLinksHere/F5,_Inc. \"List of all English Wikipedia pages containing links to this page [j]\")\n* [Related changes](/wiki/Special:RecentChangesLinked/F5,_Inc. \"Recent changes in pages linked from this page [k]\")\n* [Upload file](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:File_Upload_Wizard \"Upload files [u]\")\n* [Permanent link](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&oldid=1382418779 \"Permanent link to this revision of this page\")\n* [Page information](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=info \"More information about this page\")\n* [Cite this page](/w/index.php?title=Special:CiteThisPage&page=F5%2C_Inc.&id=1382418779&wpFormIdentifier=titleform \"Information on how to cite this page\")\n* [Get shortened URL](/w/index.php?title=Special:UrlShortener&url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FF5%2C_Inc.)\n* [Switch to legacy parser](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&useparsoid=0)\n\nPrint/export\n\n* [Download as PDF](/w/index.php?title=Special:DownloadAsPdf&page=F5%2C_Inc.&action=show-download-screen \"Download this page as a PDF file\")\n* [Printable version](javascript:print(); \"Printable version of this page [p]\")\n\nIn other projects\n\n* [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:F5_Networks)\n* [Wikidata item](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:EntityPage/Q3029296 \"Structured data on this page hosted by Wikidata [g]\")\n\nAppearance\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nU.S. information technology company\n\n|  |\n| --- |\n| This article **contains [promotional content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:What_Wikipedia_is_not#Wikipedia_is_not_a_soapbox_or_means_of_promotion \"Wikipedia:What Wikipedia is not\")**. Please help [improve it](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit) by removing [promotional language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Spam \"Wikipedia:Spam\") and inappropriate [external links](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:External_links#Advertising_and_conflicts_of_interest \"Wikipedia:External links\"), and by adding encyclopedic text written from a [neutral point of view](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Neutral_point_of_view \"Wikipedia:Neutral point of view\"). See our [advice if the article is about you](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:About_you \"Wikipedia:About you\") and read our [scam warning](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Scam_warning \"Wikipedia:Scam warning\") in case someone asks for money to edit this article. *(March 2025)* *([Learn how and when to remove this message](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Maintenance_template_removal \"Help:Maintenance template removal\"))* |\n\n| F5, Inc. |\n| --- |\n| Headquarters at [F5 Tower](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F5_Tower \"F5 Tower\") |\n| Formerly | * F5 Labs, Inc. (1996–1999) * F5 Networks, Inc. (1999–2021) |\n| Type | [Public](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_company \"Public company\") |\n| [Traded as](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ticker_symbol \"Ticker symbol\") | * [Nasdaq](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasdaq \"Nasdaq\"): [FFIV](https://www.nasdaq.com/market-activity/stocks/ffiv) * [S&P 500](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S&P_500 \"S&P 500\") component |\n| Industry | [Technology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technology \"Technology\") |\n| Founded | February 26, 1996; 30 years ago (1996-02-26) |\n| Headquarters | [F5 Tower](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F5_Tower \"F5 Tower\") [Seattle, Washington](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seattle,_Washington \"Seattle, Washington\"), U.S. |\n| Key people | François Locoh-Donou ([president](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_(corporate_title) \"President (corporate title)\") and [CEO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_executive_officer \"Chief executive officer\")) |\n| Products | [Application Security and Delivery](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_network \"Computer network\") |\n| Revenue | Increase [US$](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_dollar \"United States dollar\")3.09 billion (2025) |\n| [Operating income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earnings_before_interest_and_taxes \"Earnings before interest and taxes\") | Increase US$766 million (2025) |\n| [Net income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_income \"Net income\") | Increase US$692 million (2025) |\n| [Total assets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asset \"Asset\") | Increase US$6.32 billion (2025) |\n| [Total equity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equity_(finance) \"Equity (finance)\") | Increase US$3.59 billion (2025) |\n| Number of employees | 6,578 (2025) |\n| [ASN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomous_System_Number \"Autonomous System Number\") | * [35280](https://bgp.tools/as/35280) |\n|  |\n| Website | [f5.com](https://f5.com/) |\n| **Footnotes** Financials as of September 30, 2025[[update]](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit).[[1]](#cite_note-10K-1) |\n\n**F5, Inc.** is an American technology company specializing in multicloud [application delivery](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Application_delivery \"Application delivery\"), API security, online fraud prevention, zero trust access, and enterprise [AI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence \"Artificial intelligence\") workload security.[[2]](#cite_note-FierceNetEMAADSP2-2)\n\nWhile F5 originally developed hardware-based [application delivery controller](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Application_delivery_controller \"Application delivery controller\") (ADC) load-balancing appliances, the company has transitioned to a software-led business model.[[3]](#cite_note-UmbrexF5Strategy2-3) Software subscriptions and cloud-delivered services now account for a major portion of its product revenue, driven by a corporate shift toward predictable, recurring subscription models.[[4]](#cite_note-BarchartShift2-4) The technological core of F5's platform portfolio is its Traffic Management Operating System (TMOS). F5 offers these services as virtual machines (branded as the BIG-IP Virtual Edition) that deploy programmatically across hybrid, multi-cloud, on-premises, and network edge environments to manage traffic, secure public APIs, and enforce zero-trust identity policies.[[5]](#cite_note-NetworkWorldControl2-5)\n\n## History\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: History\")]\n\nF5, Inc., founded as F5 Networks, was established by Jeff Hussey in 1996.[[6]](#cite_note-6) In June 1999, F5 went public through an [initial public offering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Initial_public_offering \"Initial public offering\"), listing on the [NASDAQ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASDAQ \"NASDAQ\") stock exchange under the ticker symbol \"FFIV.\"[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")*]\n\nF5's modern transition culminated in major expansions into application security and software services, supporting over 23,000 customers globally, including 48 of the Fortune 50 companies, who utilize the platform for hybrid multi-cloud application delivery, zero-trust network access, and API protection.[[7]](#cite_note-BleepingCompF5-7)\n\n## Products\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Products\")]\n\n[![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/F5_spokane_2.jpg/250px-F5_spokane_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:F5_spokane_2.jpg)\n\nF5 site in [Liberty Lake](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty_Lake,_Washington \"Liberty Lake, Washington\"), [Washington state](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_state \"Washington state\"), December 2021\n\n### F5 AI Security Platform\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: F5 AI Security Platform\")]\n\nIn June 2026, F5 introduced the F5 AI Security Platform, an enterprise security offering designed to govern, discover, test, and protect artificial intelligence applications, models, and autonomous software agents.[[8]](#cite_note-SecBriefLaunch-8) The platform is built to operate across hybrid multi-cloud, on-premises, and air-gapped environments, targeting security challenges associated with agentic AI, such as prompt injection, data exfiltration, and excessive agent autonomy.[[9]](#cite_note-MSSPAlertLaunch-9)\n\nThe platform's primary security capabilities are delivered through two components integrated into F5's portfolio:[[10]](#cite_note-TechzineLaunch-10)\n\n* **F5 AI Red Team:** An automated adversarial testing system that simulates attacks on AI applications to identify vulnerabilities, such as jailbreak vulnerabilities and model data poisoning, prior to production deployment.[[11]](#cite_note-NetWorldExtensions-11)\n* **F5 AI Guardrails:** A real-time, inline semantic firewall that inspects user prompts and model-generated responses. It is designed to block injection attacks, filter out off-topic or toxic responses, and apply dynamic data loss prevention (DLP) parameters to prevent sensitive data leakage.[[11]](#cite_note-NetWorldExtensions-11)[[10]](#cite_note-TechzineLaunch-10)\n\n### F5 Application Delivery and Security Platform (ADSP)\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: F5 Application Delivery and Security Platform (ADSP)\")]\n\nIn 2025, F5 introduced the Application Delivery and Security Platform (ADSP), a unified offering that combines application delivery, API protection, and threat mitigation across hardware, software, and [Software-as-a-Service](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_as_a_service \"Software as a service\") (SaaS) environments. ADSP is designed to support hybrid and multicloud deployments and includes capabilities such as load balancing, web application firewalls, DDoS protection, and encrypted traffic inspection.[[12]](#cite_note-12)\n\n### F5 BIG-IP\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: F5 BIG-IP\")]\n\nF5's BIG-IP product family comprises hardware, modularized software, and [virtual appliances](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_appliance \"Virtual appliance\") that run the F5 TMOS operating system.[[13]](#cite_note-13)[[14]](#cite_note-embedded-tmos-14) Depending on the appliance selected, one or more BIG-IP product modules can be added.\n\nIn 1997, the company introduced its flagship product, BIG-IP.\n\n#### BIG-IP history\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=6 \"Edit section: BIG-IP history\")]\n\nOn September 7, 2004, F5 Networks released version 9.0 of the BIG-IP software in addition to appliances to run the software. Version 9.0 also marked the introduction of the company's TMOS architecture,[[15]](#cite_note-15) with enhancements including:\n\n* The move from [BSD](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution \"Berkeley Software Distribution\") to [Linux](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux \"Linux\") to handle system management functions (disks, logging, bootup, console access, etc.)\n* The creation of a Traffic Management Microkernel (TMM) to directly talk to the networking hardware and handle all network activities.[[14]](#cite_note-embedded-tmos-14)[[16]](#cite_note-16)[[17]](#cite_note-17)\n\n### F5 NGINX\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=7 \"Edit section: F5 NGINX\")]\n\nSince the NGINX, Inc. acquisition in 2019, F5 has offered an enterprise offering of [NGINX](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nginx \"Nginx\") with advanced features, multiple support [SLAs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Service-level_agreement \"Service-level agreement\"), and regular software updates.[[18]](#cite_note-18)\n\n### F5 Distributed Cloud Services\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: F5 Distributed Cloud Services\")]\n\nDuring F5 Agility 2022, F5 announced a new product offering being built on the platforms of BIG-IP, Shape Security, and Volterra.[[19]](#cite_note-CSOOnlineSaaSLaunch-19) The primary offering in this suite is the SaaS-based web application and API protection (WAAP).[[19]](#cite_note-CSOOnlineSaaSLaunch-19) F5 Distributed Cloud Services enable organizations to deploy, secure, and manage their applications across various environments, including data centers, multi-cloud setups, and the network or enterprise edge.[[20]](#cite_note-HPEGreenLakeEdge-20)\n\n## Acquisitions\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=9 \"Edit section: Acquisitions\")]\n\n### SurePath AI\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=10 \"Edit section: SurePath AI\")]\n\nIn June 2026, F5 acquired Denver-based startup SurePath AI, a platform specializing in network-based artificial intelligence discovery and shadow AI detection.[[21]](#cite_note-GeekWireSurePath-21) The acquisition's technology identifies corporate workforce usage of unauthorized AI tools and tracks agent behaviors at the network layer without requiring direct application integrations.[[22]](#cite_note-SecBoulevardSurePath-22) The financial terms of the transaction were not disclosed.[[21]](#cite_note-GeekWireSurePath-21)\n\n### CalypsoAI\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=11 \"Edit section: CalypsoAI\")]\n\nIn September 2025, F5 announced its intent to acquire CalypsoAI, a Dublin-based startup specializing in runtime AI security and adaptive guardrails, for approximately $180 million.[[23]](#cite_note-GeekWireCalypso2-23) CalypsoAI’s platform offers techniques such as red teaming, inference-layer defenses, and policy enforcement suited for generative and agentic AI workloads. On September 29, 2025, F5 completed its acquisition of CalypsoAI and introduced F5 AI Guardrails and F5 AI Red Team.\n\n### MantisNet, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=12 \"Edit section: MantisNet, Inc.\")]\n\nIn August 2025, F5 acquired MantisNet, Inc., a Reston-based provider of eBPF-powered, cloud-native network observability and real-time network intelligence.[[23]](#cite_note-GeekWireCalypso2-23) MantisNet’s agentless telemetry technology enables visibility into encrypted, ephemeral, and east–west traffic within containerized environments and 5G architectures without performance overhead, streamlining observability and security functions in cloud-native deployments.\n\n### Fletch, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=13 \"Edit section: Fletch, Inc.\")]\n\nIn June 2025, F5 acquired Fletch, Inc., a San Francisco cybersecurity startup founded in 2020 that developed agentic AI tools for threat detection and alert prioritization.[[24]](#cite_note-SecWeekMay20252-24) Fletch’s technology was designed to analyze large volumes of threat intelligence and reduce alert fatigue by surfacing relevant security signals in real time, aiding operator decision-making.\n\n### LeakSignal\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=14 \"Edit section: LeakSignal\")]\n\nIn March 2025, F5, Inc. acquired LeakSignal, a cybersecurity company specializing in real-time data protection and governance for AI applications. The acquisition aimed to enhance the F5 Application Delivery and Security Platform (ADSP) with capabilities such as AI-driven data classification, policy enforcement, and compliance monitoring. LeakSignal had been recognized by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) for its innovations in securing data in transit.[[25]](#cite_note-SecWeekMarch20252-25)\n\n### Heyhack\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=15 \"Edit section: Heyhack\")]\n\nIn March 2024, F5 announced it had acquired Heyhack ApS, a Denmark-based security penetration SaaS company. The financial details of this acquisition were not disclosed. F5 announced Heyhack's automated security reconnaissance and penetration testing solution would be rolled into the F5 Distributed Cloud Services platform.[[26]](#cite_note-SecWeekMarch20242-26)\n\n### Wib Security, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=16 \"Edit section: Wib Security, Inc.\")]\n\nIn February 2024 at AppWorld, F5's application security conference in San Jose, California, F5 announced it had acquired Wib: an API security company based in Tel Aviv, Israel, focused on vulnerability detection and observability in application development.[[27]](#cite_note-SecWeekFeb20242-27) The financial details of the acquisition were not disclosed. As of February 2024, F5 was adding the Wib API intellectual property to the F5 Distributed Cloud Services product line.\n\n### Suborbital Software Systems, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=17 \"Edit section: Suborbital Software Systems, Inc.\")]\n\nIn July 2023, Suborbital Software Systems, Inc., a technology startup that aimed to build cloud-native platforms on [WebAssembly](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WebAssembly \"WebAssembly\"), announced it had been acquired by F5, Inc.[[28]](#cite_note-SDXCentralSub2-28) The details of this acquisition were not disclosed due to the small nature of Suborbital's operations. The entire Suborbital product was deployed within the F5 Distributed Cloud Services line.\n\n### Lilac Cloud, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=18 \"Edit section: Lilac Cloud, Inc.\")]\n\nIn January 2023, F5 announced it had entered into an agreement to purchase Lilac Cloud, an application services delivery provider.[[29]](#cite_note-BuiltInLilac2-29) Lilac Cloud, based in Cupertino, California, served as F5's Content Delivery Network (CDN) provider for its Distributed Cloud Services. The entire Lilac Cloud offering was rolled into the overall F5 Distributed Cloud Services product line.\n\n### Threat Stack, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=19 \"Edit section: Threat Stack, Inc.\")]\n\nIn October 2021, F5 acquired Threat Stack, Inc., a [Boston](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boston \"Boston\") [cloud computing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloud_computing \"Cloud computing\") security [startup company](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Startup_company \"Startup company\") for a reported $68 million.[42][[30]](#cite_note-SecWeekThreat2-30) As of December 15, 2022, the previous Threat Stack offering had been rolled into the F5 Distributed Cloud platform.[[30]](#cite_note-SecWeekThreat2-30)\n\n### Volterra, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=20 \"Edit section: Volterra, Inc.\")]\n\nIn January 2021, F5 acquired Volterra, Inc., an edge networking company that sells SaaS security services, for $500 million.[40] The previously Volterra-branded products were incorporated into F5 Distributed Cloud Services, as of 2022.[41]\n\n### Shape Security, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=21 \"Edit section: Shape Security, Inc.\")]\n\nIn January 2020, F5 acquired Shape Security, Inc., an [AI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence \"Artificial intelligence\")-based bot detection company, for $1 billion.[38] F5 Networks then used the acquisition to introduce a new fraud detection engine.[39]\n\n### NGINX, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=22 \"Edit section: NGINX, Inc.\")]\n\nIn March 2019, F5 acquired [NGINX, Inc.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nginx \"Nginx\"), a company responsible for widely used open-source web server software, for $670 million.[33] On 18 January 2022, original NGINX developer [Igor Sysoev](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Igor_Sysoev \"Igor Sysoev\") left F5, Inc. Later on, forks maintaining open-source licenses such as Angie and freenginx started being developed by core NGINX contributor Maxim Dounin.[[31]](#cite_note-InfoQNGINX2-31)[34][35] Igor Sysoev is not actively involved in these projects.[36][37]\n\n### Defense.Net, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=23 \"Edit section: Defense.Net, Inc.\")]\n\nIn May 2014, F5, Inc. (then F5 Networks, Inc.) announced it had acquired Defense.Net, Inc., a company offering cloud-based DDoS mitigation services.[[32]](#cite_note-TechCrunchDefense2-32) The Defense.Net product would later become F5 Silverline. As of December 2022, the Silverline brand was retired, and the managed service offerings Silverline offered were merged into the F5 Distributed Cloud Services product portfolio.\n\n### LineRate Systems, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=24 \"Edit section: LineRate Systems, Inc.\")]\n\nIn February 2013, F5 Networks acquired LineRate Systems, a developer of software-defined networking (SDN) services, to enhance its application-layer intelligence within software-defined data centers.[[33]](#cite_note-MergrLineRate2-33) This acquisition provided F5 with access to LineRate's layer 7+ networking services technology, intellectual property, and engineering talent.\n\n### Acopia Networks, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=25 \"Edit section: Acopia Networks, Inc.\")]\n\nIn August 2007, F5, Inc., at the time, F5 Networks, Inc., announced they acquired Acopia Networks, Inc. to add file-area networking to the F5 BIG-IP application-delivery product line, also known as the Local Traffic Manager (LTM) module on the BIG-IP platforms. The deal was valued at $210 million.\n\n### Swan Labs Corporation\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=26 \"Edit section: Swan Labs Corporation\")]\n\nIn September 2005, F5 announced they had acquired Swan Labs for a total of $43 million to incorporate WAN optimization and application acceleration technology into the BIG-IP platform, specifically to improve their load balancing offering.\n\n### Traffix Systems, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=27 \"Edit section: Traffix Systems, Inc.\")]\n\nIn 2005, F5 Networks, Inc. at the time, now F5, Inc., announced it has acquired Traffix Systems for a total value of $135 million. Traffix Systems product was integrated within the F5 BIG-IP Local Traffic Manager (LTM) product line.\n\n### MagniFire Websystems, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=28 \"Edit section: MagniFire Websystems, Inc.\")]\n\nF5 acquired MagniFire Websystems in 2004 for a total cash deal size of $29 million. This acquisition allowed F5 to enter the web application security space within the BIG-IP platform. MagniFire Websystems products were sold independently when the deal first closed, then were quickly bundled into the BIG-IP product group.[[34]](#cite_note-LightReadingMagniFire-34)\n\n### uRoam, Inc.\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=29 \"Edit section: uRoam, Inc.\")]\n\nF5 acquired uRoam, Inc. in 2003 for a total cash deal size of $25 million. The strategic intent behind F5 Networks' acquisition of uRoam was to diversify its product offerings beyond its core application delivery and security services. This move aimed to address the growing demand for secure remote access to corporate networks and applications, aligning with industry trends.[[35]](#cite_note-FundingUniverseHistory-35)\n\n## References\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=F5,_Inc.&action=edit&section=30 \"Edit section: References\")]\n\n1. [↑](#cite_ref-10K_1-0) [\"F5, Inc. 2025 Annual Report (Form 10-K)\"](https://www.sec.gov/ix?doc=/Archives/edgar/data/1048695/000104869525000157/ffiv-20250930.htm). [U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Securities_and_Exchange_Commission \"U.S. Securiti